            <ArticleTitle>Conservative treatment of ectopic pregnancy in a cesarean scar.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>869-71</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Pregnancy developing in a cesarean scar is a very rare but possibly life-threatening condition because of the risk of rupture and excessive hemorrhage.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 34-year-old woman presented with lower abdominal pain at 6 weeks of gestation. A cesarean delivery had been performed 3 years earlier. Transvaginal ultrasound examination revealed a viable pregnancy developing in the anterior wall of the uterus. The patient was treated successfully with systemic methotrexate and curettage.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Conservative management with methotrexate and curettage can be considered in the treatment of ectopic cesarean scar pregnancy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
